    "q-37587": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37587",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$\n\nIII. The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum of $f$",
      "worked_solution": "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})={({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0,$ the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
