    "q-104974": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104974",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(2,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(2,-1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(2,-1)$ is a local minimum of $f$",
      "worked_solution": "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-4)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=24>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-4<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(2,-1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)<0,$ the point $(2,-1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
